---
import DocShell from '../../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'install', label: 'Install' },
   { id: 'fixture', label: 'The sr fixture' },
   { id: 'queue', label: 'The command queue' },
   { id: 'timeouts', label: 'Timeouts' },
   { id: 'matchers', label: 'Matchers' },
   { id: 'example', label: 'Example test file' },
   { id: 'browser', label: 'Vitest browser mode' },
   { id: 'other-runners', label: 'Other runners' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Test with Vitest"
   summary="a11ied/vitest gives Vitest a test whose sr fixture is a queued screen reader, so a test writes no await until it needs a value. It needs Vitest 4.0.0 or later."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="panel" id="install">
      <h2>Install</h2>
      <pre><code>npm install --save-dev a11ied vitest</code></pre>
      <p>
         Vitest is an optional peer dependency of <code>a11ied</code>, range
         <code>&gt;=4.0.0</code>, so the package installs and runs without it.
         <code>a11ied/vitest</code> exports <code>test</code> and <code>it</code> with the
         <code>sr</code> fixture, the three <code>expect</code> matchers, and everything
         <code>a11ied/test</code> exports.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="fixture">
      <h2>The sr fixture</h2>
      <p>
         The exported <code>test</code> provides <code>sr</code>, a
         <code>QueuedScreenReader</code>. The fixture starts it from the <code>srOptions</code>
         fixture before each test and stops it after the test, whether the test passed or
         failed. Override the options for a file with
         <code>test.extend({'{'} srOptions: {'{'} url {'}'} {'}'})</code>, or for a block with
         <code>test.scoped</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>import {'{'} test {'}'} from 'a11ied/vitest';

const checkout = test.extend({'{'}
   srOptions: {'{'} url: 'http://localhost:3000/checkout' {'}'},
{'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>srOptions</code> takes the <code>ScreenReaderOptions</code> fields:
         <code>sr</code> (<code>virtual</code> by default, <code>voiceover</code>, or
         <code>nvda</code>), a <code>url</code> or inline <code>html</code>, <code>mode</code>,
         <code>engine</code>, <code>recordingPath</code>, <code>timeoutMs</code>, and
         <code>idleTimeoutMinutes</code>. The default mode, <code>in-process</code>, runs the
         screen reader inside the test process, so tests in parallel workers do not compete for
         the one active <code>a1 sr</code> session.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="queue">
      <h2>The command queue</h2>
      <p>
         A method call queues a command and returns at once. The queued commands run one at a
         time in the order they were queued. A test calls the methods with no
         <code>await</code>.
      </p>
      <p>
         Await a call when the test needs its value, such as
         <code>const item = await sr.read();</code>. That await runs every command queued before
         it, then that call, and gives its value.
      </p>
      <p>
         Each call returns a chain with every method on it, so commands and checks may follow
         one another with dots. <code>expect</code> starts a check, and <code>and</code> adds
         another. The chain checks what <code>expectSpoken</code>,
         <code>expectSpokenInOrder</code>, and <code>expectCursorOn</code> check, under the
         names <code>spoken</code>, <code>spokenInOrder</code>, and <code>cursorOn</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>sr.goTo({'{'} role: 'button', name: 'Is it unstyled?' {'}'})
   .checkpoint('enter')
   .press('Enter')
   .expect.spoken(/expanded$/u, {'{'} since: 'enter' {'}'})
   .and.cursorOn({'{'} role: 'button', name: 'Is it unstyled?' {'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         A check waits. It reads the reader again every 100 ms until it passes or
         <code>timeoutMs</code> runs out, 5000 ms by default, so an announcement or a focus move
         that lands a moment after the key that caused it still counts, and a test needs no pause
         before it. Pass <code>timeoutMs: 0</code> to check once. A check with <code>not</code>
         never waits: absence now is its answer. A failure after waiting says how long it waited.
      </p>
      <p>
         Await a call the test expects to fail, such as
         <code>await expect(sr.expectSpoken('Refund')).rejects.toThrow('Refund');</code>. The
         rejection goes to that await and the queue runs the next command.
      </p>
      <p>
         When a command throws and nothing awaited it, every command queued after it is skipped
         and rejects with the same error, and the test fails. The fixture records each queued
         command on the test task. After the test function returns, Vitest awaits those and
         reports each rejection as an error of the test, with the message and a code frame at
         the line that queued the command. The error keeps its class,
         <code>ScreenReaderAssertionError</code> for a failed check, its <code>message</code>,
         and its <code>expected</code>, <code>phrases</code>, and <code>exitCode</code> fields.
      </p>
      <p>
         A failed check in a test over a native <code>&lt;select&gt;</code> labeled Select country,
         run with the simulated screen reader, prints:
      </p>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>FAIL  combobox.test.ts > combobox component > announces active option and expanded state
ScreenReaderAssertionError: "option, Canada, 1 of 3" was not announced in the transcript (5 phrases checked).
The reader said:
  "document"
  "main"
  "Select country"
  "combobox, Select country, Australia, has popup listbox, not expanded"
  "option, Australia, not selected, position 1, set size 3"
 > combobox.test.ts:23:10
     21|       sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country');
     22|       sr.next();
     23|       sr.expectSpoken('option, Canada, 1 of 3');
       |          ^</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="timeouts">
      <h2>Timeouts</h2>
      <p>
         Queued commands run inside Vitest's <code>testTimeout</code>, 5000 ms by default, and
         before <code>afterEach</code>. A test that queues a long sequence needs a longer
         <code>testTimeout</code>. Pass it as the third argument to one test, or set it in
         <code>vitest.config.ts</code> for the run. <code>timeoutMs</code> in
         <code>srOptions</code> bounds each single command.
      </p>
      <p>
         When the test times out or the run is canceled, Vitest aborts the test's signal and the
         queue skips every command not yet started. The command already running finishes on its
         own.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="matchers">
      <h2>Matchers</h2>
      <p>
         Importing <code>a11ied/vitest</code> installs three matchers on <code>expect</code> and
         adds their types.
      </p>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row"><code>toHaveSpoken(match, {'{'} since, not {'}'})</code><span>An announcement matches. The failure message is the one <code>expectSpoken</code> throws.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>toHaveSpokenInOrder([a, b, c])</code><span>Each match is found after the previous one, with any announcements between. The failure says which match it did not find and which announcement it looked for after.</span></div>
         <div class="route-row"><code>toHaveCursorOn({'{'} role, name {'}'})</code><span>The element under the cursor has the role, the name, or both. The failure says which element the cursor is on.</span></div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Given a <code>ScreenReader</code> or a <code>QueuedScreenReader</code>, each matcher
         reads the transcript first, so it returns a promise: await it. Given an array of
         announcements, transcript entries, or the element <code>read()</code> returned, it
         answers at once. The matchers check once. The reader methods
         <code>expectSpoken</code>, <code>expectCursorOn</code>, and
         <code>expectSpokenInOrder</code> check the same things without an <code>await</code>,
         and wait for them.
      </p>
      <p>
         For an announcement the page makes later, pass the transcript to
         <code>expect.poll</code>. It retries the matcher until Vitest's poll timeout, 1000 ms by
         default, and takes the <code>timeout</code> and <code>interval</code> options.
         <code>sr.wait({'{'} for {'}'})</code> does the same from the reader.
      </p>
      <pre><code>await expect.poll(() => sr.transcript()).toHaveSpoken('Draft saved');</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="example">
      <h2>Example test file</h2>
      <p>
         The page is a combobox labeled Select country with three options, Canada first. Arrow
         Down from the collapsed combobox expands it and moves to the first option. The dev
         server runs on port 3000.
      </p>
      <pre><code>// combobox.test.ts
import {'{'} describe, expect {'}'} from 'vitest';
import {'{'} test {'}'} from 'a11ied/vitest';

const combobox = test.extend({'{'}
   srOptions: {'{'} url: 'http://localhost:3000/components/combobox' {'}'},
{'}'});

describe('combobox component', () => {'{'}
   combobox('announces active option and expanded state', ({'{'} sr {'}'}) => {'{'}
      sr.press('Tab');
      sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country, collapsed');

      sr.press('ArrowDown');
      sr.expectSpoken('combobox, Select country, expanded');
      sr.expectSpoken('option, Canada, 1 of 3');
   {'}'});

   combobox('reports the option under the cursor', async ({'{'} sr {'}'}) => {'{'}
      sr.press('Tab');
      sr.press('ArrowDown');

      const item = await sr.read();

      expect(item).toMatchObject({'{'} role: 'option', name: 'Canada' {'}'});
   {'}'});
{'}'});</code></pre>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="browser">
      <h2>Vitest browser mode</h2>
      <p>
         In Vitest browser mode the test already runs in a real browser, so
         <code>a11ied/browser</code> starts the simulated screen reader on that page. There is no
         separate process, no second page load, and no <code>open()</code>: mount the component, then read it.
         The reader covers the document body, or <code>container</code>.
      </p>
      <pre><code>// vitest.config.ts
import {'{'} playwright {'}'} from '@vitest/browser-playwright';
import {'{'} defineConfig {'}'} from 'vitest/config';

export default defineConfig({'{'}
   test: {'{'}
      browser: {'{'}
         enabled: true,
         provider: playwright(),
         instances: [{'{'} browser: 'chromium' {'}'}],
      {'}'},
   {'}'},
{'}'});</code></pre>
      <pre><code>// pay-button.browser.test.ts
import {'{'} test {'}'} from 'a11ied/browser';
import {'{'} render {'}'} from 'vitest-browser-react';
import {'{'} PayButton {'}'} from './pay-button';

test('announces the price', ({'{'} sr {'}'}) => {'{'}
   render(&lt;PayButton amount="12.00" /&gt;);

   sr.next('button');
   sr.expectCursorOn({'{'} role: 'button', name: 'Pay 12.00' {'}'});
{'}'});</code></pre>
      <p>
         The exported <code>test</code> provides an <code>sr</code> fixture over the document
         body, a <code>QueuedScreenReader</code> like the one in <code>a11ied/vitest</code>, and
         <code>a11ied/browser</code> installs the same three matchers. A test that starts its own
         reader over one mounted component calls
         <code>queuedScreenReader({'{'} container {'}'})</code>, or <code>screenReader({'{'} container {'}'})</code>
         for the unqueued <code>ScreenReader</code>.
         <code>@vitest/browser-playwright</code> needs a Chromium that
         <code>npx playwright install chromium</code> provides.
      </p>
      <p>
         Two methods are not available in browser mode. <code>open()</code> throws, because the
         page is the test's own. <code>perform()</code> throws too, because the command registry
         that resolves names loads Guidepup's Node-only tables. Every typed method is available.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="other-runners">
      <h2>Other runners</h2>
      <p>
         Under Jest, Mocha, Playwright Test, or a script, <code>a11ied/test</code> exports
         <code>queuedScreenReader</code> and <code>screenReader</code> and depends on no test
         runner. The queue rules above are the same there. <code>await using</code>, or
         <code>await sr.stop()</code> in the cleanup hook, runs every queued command, stops the
         reader, and rethrows a failure nobody awaited. See the
         <a href="/guides/testing">testing guide</a>.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
